// A transition whose target TAIL-CALLS ANOTHER MACHINE has no route. Reduced
// from the corpus fixtures at `scalar field store sequence: write frame
// agreement`, found by attributing the macOS arm64 native roster.
//
//   transition { _ -> self.pick(1) }   (another machine)   OMITTED
//   transition { _ -> done() }         (a state here)      checks
//   self.pick(1); transition { _ -> done() }               checks
//
// So it is neither calling another machine nor transitioning -- it is the two
// together, a cross-machine call in TARGET position. Parameters are
// irrelevant: the bare no-argument form omits too.
//
// THE LABEL FLIPS WITH AN UNRELATED STATEMENT, which is the sharpest
// demonstration of the phase-label hazard this corpus has produced. The same
// program reports:
//
//   with `self.n = 1;` before the transition
//                  -> `scalar field store sequence: write frame agreement`
//   without it     -> `statement sequence: call count agreement`
//
// Adding a field store that the transition does not touch renames the
// failure. Those two phases are ONE gap, and anyone triaging by string will
// file them as two. That also means the `write frame agreement` group in the
// native roster is not about write frames.
//
// NOT ALL of that group is this, though. Three of its six fixtures carry a
// cross-machine transition target -- `collections/runtime_frame_indexed_param_read_exit`,
// `slices/domained_runtime_end_subslice_exit`,
// `termination/runtime_shrinking_slice_recursion_exit` -- and three do not:
// `wire/runtime_wire_decode_byte_slice_exit`,
// `wire/runtime_wire_utf8_edge_verdicts_exit`,
// `filesystem/dir_walk_wrappers_exit`. Those three are UNATTRIBUTED; do not
// assume this fixture covers them.
//
// One caution for the repair: `runtime_frame_indexed_param_read_exit` also
// passes a fixed array BY VALUE to the called machine, which looks like
// BOUNDARY-PRIMITIVE-ARRAY-PARAMETER. It is not -- the array is not what
// carries this. The identical program with no array parameter at all omits
// just the same, which is the control that separates them.
use omega_language_std::console;
use omega::language::core::binding;

data Main {
    console: Binding<Console>;
    count: i32;
}

machine Main::main(&mut self) {
    self.count = 1;
    transition { _ -> self.finish() }
}

machine Main::finish(&mut self) reaches Console {
    transition { _ -> done() }
    state done(&mut self) { self.console.exit_process(0); }
}
